# Standard design patterns for maintainable code

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 cross-cutting design rule.

## Use This When

- Apply this on any non-trivial implementation or refactor where branching, responsibilities, or extension points are growing.
- Especially relevant for workers, integration boundaries, orchestration code, adapters, admin flows, and reusable UI/domain modules.

## Core Rule

- Prefer standard, recognizable design patterns over ad hoc branching once complexity grows.
- Favor patterns that reduce conditional sprawl and isolate change: `strategy + registry`, `adapter`, `factory`, `template method`, `policy object`, `builder`, or well-scoped composition.
- Name abstractions by responsibility, not by vague framework terms (`channel handler`, `share policy`, `transport adapter`, not `manager2` or `processorX`).

## Required behavior

- Keep orchestration code thin. Channel-specific, provider-specific, or mode-specific behavior should live behind a stable interface.
- Add abstractions only when they simplify the codebase. Do not introduce ceremonial layers with no concrete benefit.
- Prefer extending a registry or adding a new strategy over editing a long chain of `if` / `switch` branches in core flows.
- Keep pattern boundaries testable: validate the seam where the orchestrator selects a strategy, adapter, or policy.
- Reuse established patterns already present in the repository before inventing new internal frameworks. Reuse-first is a pre-step for ALL artifacts (not just patterns) — see `/.cursor/rules/reuse-first-discovery.mdc`.

## Anti-patterns

- Growing a central coordinator by appending more channel/type/provider `if` branches.
- Mixing transport details, payload normalization, and business decisions in one function.
- Creating custom pseudo-patterns when a common, named pattern already fits.
- Hiding simple logic behind unnecessary indirection when one small helper would do.

## Feature-component reuse (render layer)

Recognizable design patterns apply to FEATURE components, not just primitives: one
parameterizable renderer, every cross-cutting concern a prop/slot, each variant a config.
When a task adds a "new view", the pattern is "add a config + inject the new provider,"
NEVER "add a parallel renderer." Re-deriving an existing list/tree/card/cell renderer is
the anti-pattern this rule forbids.
